const zhuXiSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('orientation-label', 'Zhu Xi and the later map called “Neo-Confucianism”', [
    'Zhu Xi (1130–1200) was a Southern Song scholar, teacher, commentator, and official whose synthesis became enormously influential across East Asia. “Neo-Confucian” is a modern English umbrella, not his own denominational title. Zhu usually described the project as daoxue, Learning of the Way, and later classifications also speak of lixue, Learning of Pattern, or the Cheng–Zhu lineage. Those labels gather thinkers who disagreed about texts, cultivation, politics, and cosmology. They should orient a reader without implying that a single school appeared fully formed or that Zhu merely repeated an already settled doctrine.',
    'His ambition joined tasks that modern departments often separate. He interpreted the classics, trained moral attention, explained human nature and emotion, debated Buddhism and Daoism, investigated patterns in natural and social affairs, and built institutions for learning. The result was not a detachable metaphysical system sitting above ethical life. Claims about li, often translated as pattern or principle, and qi, psychophysical stuff or vital material force, mattered because people had to understand why a shared capacity for goodness appears amid partial, turbulent lives. Philosophy was a practice of reading, inquiry, ritual, reflection, and action directed toward becoming reliably humane.',
  ], ['confucius', 'mencius', 'wang-yangming'], ['The Four Books', 'Reflections on Things at Hand']),
  section('life-song-world', 'A learned life in a fractured Song world', [
    'Born in Fujian in 1130, Zhu Xi grew up after the Northern Song court had lost the north to the Jurchen Jin. His father and teachers belonged to networks that connected classical learning with arguments about public responsibility. Zhu passed the highest civil-service examination as a young man, but his official career was intermittent. He served in local and central posts, administered relief, promoted schools and academies, memorialized the throne, and repeatedly withdrew into study and teaching. This mixture matters: his philosophy was shaped neither by an isolated contemplative life nor by uninterrupted control of policy.',
    'Zhu could be forceful and partisan. He criticized officials, championed allies and predecessors, and helped define legitimate transmission of the Way. Near the end of his life, political enemies condemned the Learning of the Way as false learning, removed him from office, and restricted his teaching network. He died in 1200 before the state elevated his commentaries into examination orthodoxy. That sequence blocks a triumphalist biography. Later authority grew from texts, students, academies, political reversals, and state decisions; it was not a status Zhu possessed without challenge while alive.',
  ], ['zhu-xi'], ['Reflections on Things at Hand']),
  section('canon-commentary', 'Commentary, curriculum, and the Four Books', [
    'Zhu Xi did not create the Analects, Mencius, Great Learning, or Doctrine of the Mean. He selected and coordinated these inherited works as the Four Books, edited the latter two out of the Record of Rites, supplied chapter divisions and commentary, and proposed an ordered curriculum. The Great Learning framed moral learning through inquiry and self-cultivation; the Analects and Mencius displayed Confucian teaching in speech and argument; the Doctrine of the Mean carried the learner toward questions of sincerity and cosmic participation. The canon was therefore an interpretive achievement, not the discovery of a self-evident ancient package.',
    'Commentary for Zhu was disciplined thinking. A gloss could settle a word, relate passages, reject a rival reading, and train the reader to inhabit a moral distinction. He revised his Four Books commentaries over decades and paired close reading with conversation and practice. After his death, Yuan authorities made the Four Books and his interpretations central to the civil-service examinations; Ming and Qing institutions continued their exceptional reach. Official adoption amplified a curriculum already sustained by teachers and print, but it could also narrow acceptable answers. Canon formation joined pedagogy, philosophical argument, and power.',
  ], ['confucius', 'mencius', 'wang-yangming'], ['Analects', 'Mencius', 'Great Learning', 'Doctrine of the Mean']),
  section('li-qi', 'Li and qi: pattern in concrete existence', [
    'Li can mean pattern, order, coherence, or principle. Zhu used it for the intelligible ways things are what they are and ought to develop: the pattern of a relationship, a living thing, or humane conduct. Qi is the psychophysical stuff, energy, or vital material force through which every concrete thing exists and changes. Translation easily misleads. Li is not automatically a Platonic Form in a separate realm, and qi is not merely inert matter. Zhu distinguishes them to explain different aspects of reality while insisting that actual things never contain one without the other.',
    'This complementarity explains both common moral capacity and particular difference. Human nature, considered as li, is good because its constitutive patterns include humaneness, rightness, ritual propriety, and wisdom. Yet each embodied person receives qi in a distinct configuration, clearer or more turbid, balanced or uneven. Turbidity does not make qi evil or remove responsibility; it names the conditions through which desires and emotions can obscure appropriate response. Cultivation does not flee embodiment to reach pure principle. It refines attention, conduct, and dispositions so that pattern becomes more completely expressed in the life one actually has.',
  ], ['mencius', 'wang-yangming'], ['Reflections on Things at Hand']),
  section('mind-nature-emotions', 'Heart-mind, nature, emotions, and desire', [
    'The xin, or heart-mind, thinks, feels, attends, and directs. Zhu’s account resists a modern division between cold reason and private emotion. Nature identifies the normative pattern constituting a human being, while the heart-mind is the living faculty through which that nature can govern perception and conduct. Feelings arise as qi is activated in encounters. Joy, anger, grief, and pleasure are not sins simply because they move; the problem is excess, deficiency, mistiming, or self-centered fixation. Proper feeling is responsive to the real features and obligations of a situation.',
    'Desire therefore needs discrimination rather than wholesale destruction. Zhu warns against selfish human desires that displace the “heavenly pattern” of relationships, but hunger, affection, and ordinary needs are not thereby condemned. The language can become dangerous if detached from his demanding account of inquiry: authorities may identify their own preferences with pattern and police others as selfish. Zhu’s answer is sustained learning, reverent attention, correction by texts and teachers, and practical testing. Whether those safeguards adequately answer institutional hierarchy remains a live critical question, not something the vocabulary of li settles by itself.',
  ], ['mencius', 'wang-yangming'], ['Reflections on Things at Hand']),
  section('cosmic-synthesis', 'The Great Ultimate and a moral cosmos', [
    'Zhu Xi interpreted Zhou Dunyi’s Diagram of the Supreme Ultimate and other Northern Song texts to connect cosmology with cultivation. The Great Ultimate, taiji, is not a creator deity standing outside the world. It names the highest integrating pattern of the ceaseless transformations of qi, articulated through yin and yang and the five phases. Zhu could say that each thing possesses the Great Ultimate because each concrete being instantiates pattern fully in its own mode. The formulation is holistic, but it does not make stones, plants, animals, and humans morally identical.',
    'Cosmology gave ethical life depth: becoming humane aligns a person with patterns operating through family, community, seasons, and the broader world. Yet modern readers should resist turning correlation into experimental science or ecological doctrine without argument. Zhu investigated astronomical, calendrical, ritual, historical, and natural questions using the learning available to him; some conclusions are obsolete. His enduring philosophical proposal is methodological: moral knowledge belongs within a world of patterned relations, not in an autonomous will. The proposal invites comparison with metaphysics and environmental ethics, but those are later conversations rather than labels he used.',
  ], ['zhu-xi'], ['Diagram of the Supreme Ultimate Explained', 'Reflections on Things at Hand']),
  section('investigation', 'Investigating things and extending knowledge', [
    'The Great Learning links extending knowledge with gewu, commonly rendered investigating things. Zhu understood “things” broadly: texts, events, institutions, natural phenomena, and the roles and relationships of ordinary life. Investigation seeks the li that makes a matter intelligible and morally significant. It may involve reading, questioning teachers, comparing cases, observing affairs, and reflecting on conduct. It is neither laboratory science before its time nor abstract deduction from fixed premises. Its cumulative promise is that disciplined attention across particulars can produce an integrated grasp of how one ought to respond.',
    'A famous later story says that the young Wang Yangming exhausted himself trying to investigate bamboo and took the failure as evidence against Zhu’s method. The anecdote has pedagogical force, but it caricatures Zhu if bamboo-watching is made the whole program. Zhu’s own conversations describe gradual study, repeated reading, and practical engagement, followed at times by a breakthrough in which accumulated insights become connected. Investigation can still appear overconfident: who decides that the pattern has been exhausted, and how are inherited hierarchies challenged? His method needs communities of correction, not a solitary declaration of completed insight.',
  ], ['wang-yangming'], ['Great Learning', 'Learning to Be a Sage']),
  section('knowledge-action', 'Knowledge, action, and reverent attentiveness', [
    'Zhu distinguishes knowing from acting without allowing either to stand alone. Knowledge guides action and in that sense comes first, but enacted practice gives learning its weight and completion. Memorizing a moral term while remaining unmoved is deficient knowledge; energetic conduct without discrimination can also go astray. The learner cycles through reading, inquiry, reflection, ritual, and correction until appropriate response becomes stable. This account supplies the background for Wang Yangming’s later insistence on their unity. Wang radicalizes a problem Zhu already recognized rather than introducing action into a purely bookish system.',
    'Jing, often translated reverence, seriousness, or reverent attentiveness, holds that cycle together. It is a gathered, alert orientation that prevents the heart-mind from scattering among impulses. Quiet-sitting could help settle attention, but Zhu resisted making stillness the exclusive path or treating ordinary affairs as contamination. Reverence operates while reading, speaking, governing, mourning, and caring for others. The ethical test is not a private feeling of serenity but greater accuracy and reliability in relationships. Cultivation is gradual and demanding because clarity must survive changing circumstances, not only the protected conditions of a study or meditation room.',
  ], ['wang-yangming'], ['Learning to Be a Sage']),
  section('buddhism-daoism', 'Learning through criticism of Buddhism and Daoism', [
    'Zhu Xi defined the Confucian Way in sustained argument with Buddhist and Daoist traditions. He criticized what he saw as Buddhist emptiness, withdrawal from family obligations, and an inward search that detached mind from patterned things. He also rejected quests for transcendence or longevity that displaced ethical relationships. These charges helped construct a Confucian identity, but they do not neutrally describe the many Buddhist and Daoist positions of Song China. Chan Buddhists, for example, also disputed quietism and trained conduct. Polemic must be read as polemic, not as a reliable survey of its targets.',
    'The boundary was intellectually porous. Zhu’s vocabulary, contemplative discipline, debates about mind, and confidence in a coherent cosmos developed in a world deeply shaped by Buddhism and Daoism. Influence need not mean covert conversion, and similarity does not erase disagreement. The more useful question asks how competitive traditions transformed shared problems: whether awakening is sudden or gradual, how desire obscures responsiveness, and whether ethical norms are discovered in mind, text, or relationship. Zhu’s synthesis is distinctively Confucian because of its classical canon and role ethics, yet its distinctiveness emerged through exchange as well as rejection.',
  ], ['wang-yangming'], ['Reflections on Things at Hand']),
  section('institutional-authority', 'From contested teacher to examination authority', [
    'Zhu’s posthumous rise was neither immediate nor purely intellectual. Students preserved conversations and editions; academies taught his curriculum; allies secured honors; printers multiplied texts; and governments found the Four Books commentaries useful for selecting officials. In 1313 the Yuan examination system formally privileged the Four Books and Zhu’s interpretations, more than a century after his death. Later dynasties altered the examinations while maintaining a powerful association between classical mastery and his commentarial framework. “Orthodox” thus describes an institutional history, not proof that every scholar freely judged his arguments superior.',
    'Authority also traveled unevenly. Korean, Japanese, and Vietnamese scholars adopted, revised, and contested Zhu learning within different political and textual settings. Even in China, rival readings persisted, and examination essays did not exhaust lived Confucian practice. Canonical status enabled broad literacy in a common discourse and sustained demanding traditions of study; it also rewarded conformity and could marginalize alternatives. A museum account should hold both facts together. Zhu Xi was neither merely a state ideologue nor an innocent thinker whose ideas happened to be used later. His own curriculum already organized legitimate learning, while later institutions greatly changed its scale and force.',
  ], ['wang-yangming'], ['The Four Books']),
  section('disputes-legacy', 'A synthesis criticized from within Confucian learning', [
    'Critics asked whether Zhu placed pattern too far outside the learner’s immediate moral awareness, burdened cultivation with endless textual investigation, or converted living judgment into scholastic authority. Lu Jiuyuan emphasized that the heart-mind itself is pattern, a position later paired with Wang Yangming’s teaching as the Lu–Wang school of mind. That lineage label highlights a real contrast, but it was systematized after the fact and can hide differences between Lu and Wang. Zhu’s followers also disagreed among themselves about mind, feelings, investigation, and the proper relation of scholarship to government.',
    'Zhu Xi’s legacy is therefore best studied as an argument-generating framework. Li and qi offered later thinkers a vocabulary for unity and difference; Four Books pedagogy reshaped education; investigation joined moral formation to knowledge of concrete affairs. The same architecture raises questions about hierarchy, official orthodoxy, gendered roles, and confidence that inherited patterns are normatively good. Modern “New Confucian” projects are later movements again, not the same thing as Song daoxue. Continuity should be demonstrated through texts and institutions rather than implied by repeating the word Confucian across eight centuries.',
  ], ['wang-yangming'], ['The Four Books', 'Reflections on Things at Hand']),
  section('reading-path', 'A reading path through text, commentary, and reception', [
    'Begin with Daniel Gardner’s Learning to Be a Sage, especially its selections on method, knowledge and action, reading, holding on, and energetic practice. Read slowly enough to see how metaphysical terms arise inside instructions to learners. Then compare Zhu’s Great Learning commentary and selected Analects glosses with the underlying classics. Ask what his arrangement makes visible, what alternatives it excludes, and how commentary changes the act of reading. The SEP and IEP entries provide maps of li, qi, nature, cultivation, and natural inquiry without requiring one English translation to carry every sense.',
    'Next place Zhu’s curriculum inside history. Gardner’s study of the Analects clarifies canon and commentary; Tillman reconstructs the contested discourse behind Zhu’s later ascendancy. Only then compare Wang Yangming, whose criticisms make more sense when investigation is not reduced to staring at bamboo. Track four distinctions throughout: Zhu’s claims versus later school formulas, conceptual li–qi distinction versus separate existence, ethical inquiry versus modern science, and posthumous orthodoxy versus lifetime authority. That sequence presents a philosopher, a pedagogy, and an institution without collapsing them into one timeless system.',
  ], ['wang-yangming', 'confucius', 'mencius'], ['Learning to Be a Sage', 'Analects', 'Great Learning']),
];

