const wangYangmingSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('orientation-name-label', 'Wang Shouren, called Yangming, on a later philosophical map', [
    'Wang Yangming (1472–1529) was the best-known name of Wang Shouren, courtesy name Bo’an, an official, military commander, teacher, and philosopher of Ming China. “Yangming” came from the name of a retreat and became the conventional scholarly name. Modern accounts place him within Neo-Confucianism and often contrast his “school of mind” with Zhu Xi’s “school of pattern.” These are useful retrospective maps. Wang inherited institutions already shaped by Zhu learning, but he did not simply found a denomination with timeless borders, and later Lu–Wang or Cheng–Zhu classifications organize arguments more neatly than historical communities always did.',
    'His central proposal is practical: moral understanding is not information that can remain inert while action waits elsewhere. The heart-mind already has liangzhi, pure or innate knowing, a capacity to recognize fitting and unfitting response. Cultivation removes the self-centered desires that prevent this capacity from becoming effective in affairs. This does not make every spontaneous feeling correct. Wang required vigilant examination of intentions, correction in relationships, and action under pressure. His claims about mind, pattern, knowing, and the unity of knowledge and action form one program, not independent motivational slogans.',
  ], ['zhu-xi', 'mencius'], ['Instructions for Practical Living', 'Inquiry on the Great Learning']),
  section('life-public-power', 'Exile, teaching, and morally ambiguous public power', [
    'Born into an official family in Zhejiang in 1472, Wang passed the metropolitan examination and held a succession of posts. In 1506 he protested the punishment of officials by the powerful eunuch Liu Jin. Wang was beaten, imprisoned, and banished to a remote station at Longchang in Guizhou. Later accounts make his 1508 awakening there a decisive realization that the resources of sagehood are present in one’s own heart-mind. The episode shaped his teaching, but biography should not convert one dramatic night into the sole cause of ideas developed through texts, teachers, administration, and continuing debate.',
    'After Liu Jin’s fall, Wang returned to office and gained fame by suppressing rebellions, defeating the prince of Ning, administering frontier regions, and teaching disciples. These were coercive state activities, not a neutral stage for moral heroism. Sources can praise strategy and benevolent administration while still asking what military pacification meant for affected communities. Wang also experienced court suspicion and disputes over credit. He died in 1529 after a final campaign. His life makes the unity of knowledge and action concrete, but it also prevents easy identification of decisive action with justice.',
  ], ['wang-yangming'], ['Instructions for Practical Living']),
  section('texts-teaching', 'Records of teaching rather than one finished system', [
    'Wang’s philosophy survives in memorials, letters, poems, commentaries, and records of conversations. The Chuanxilu, translated as Instructions for Practical Living or Record for Practice, was compiled in stages by disciples. It preserves exchanges from different periods and settings rather than a treatise written once from beginning to end. The Inquiry on the Great Learning presents a compact account of cultivation, while letters address particular students and controversies. Readers should therefore date and locate formulations where possible instead of forcing every remark into an unchanging system.',
    'Dialogue is part of the method. Wang presses students to discover where an apparently correct statement fails to move their intentions, and he adapts instruction to different dispositions. This pedagogical flexibility generated later disputes: was a radical formulation his final doctrine, medicine for one student, or one pole of a complementary teaching? Disciples edited the record and developed competing lineages after his death. Attribution to “Wang’s school” may consequently describe several receptions. The primary texts remain indispensable precisely because famous formulas lose their diagnostic setting when detached from questions and replies.',
  ], ['wang-yangming'], ['Instructions for Practical Living', 'Inquiry on the Great Learning']),
  section('mind-pattern-one-body', 'The heart-mind as pattern in a relational world', [
    'Wang radicalized the claim that the heart-mind is li, pattern, and denied that pattern should be sought as though it were an independent object outside morally engaged awareness. This is not straightforward solipsism. The heart-mind is not a private theater inventing other people; it is the responsive center through which relationships and things become ethically intelligible. A filial situation, for example, is not known by locating an abstract principle beside one’s parent. Its pattern is realized when the heart-mind accurately responds within that concrete relationship.',
    'His language of forming one body with Heaven, Earth, and the myriad things makes this relational scope explicit. Compassionate response to another person, an animal, or even damage to plants and objects can reveal participation in a larger field of concern. Degrees and duties remain; Wang does not erase every distinction. Nor does “one body” automatically yield a modern environmental or egalitarian politics. It challenges the fantasy of an isolated moral self, yet the institutions through which concern is organized require separate criticism. Mind is expansive because response reaches the world, not because an ego contains it as an idea.',
  ], ['mencius', 'zhu-xi'], ['Inquiry on the Great Learning']),
  section('innate-knowing', 'Liangzhi: pure knowing without private infallibility', [
    'Liangzhi, drawn from Mencius, is rendered innate knowing, pure knowing, or innate moral knowledge. It is the heart-mind’s immediate capacity to register moral salience: the incipient unease at wrongdoing or concern for another before a calculated theory. “Innate” does not mean a complete list of rules is consciously present at birth. It identifies a capacity whose authority does not derive solely from external reward, punishment, or textual expertise. Wang’s mature teaching centered on extending this knowing until it informs each intention and affair.',
    'Pure knowing is easily confused with sincerity of feeling. Wang denies that equation. Selfish desires distort attention, rationalization disguises convenience, and social habits can make cruelty seem normal. Cultivation examines the intention at its first movement and removes what blocks the heart-mind’s clearer response. Texts and teachers can expose error, although they do not insert morality from outside. A modern appeal to “my intuition” is therefore not automatically liangzhi. The claim is demanding and contestable: if disagreement persists, Wang needs practices of correction that distinguish awakened discernment from confident bias.',
  ], ['mencius'], ['Instructions for Practical Living']),
  section('knowledge-action', 'The unity of knowing and acting', [
    'Wang’s unity of knowing and acting attacks a familiar evasion. Someone may recite that filial care or justice is good while repeatedly refusing it, then claim that knowledge is complete and only action is missing. Wang argues that genuine moral knowing already includes an affective and motivational direction toward performance. Action is the completion and manifestation of knowing; knowing is the lucid beginning of action. The two can be distinguished in explanation, but separating them into independent stages encourages learners to shelter behind verbal correctness.',
    'The doctrine does not imply that knowing any fact causes the corresponding behavior. Wang is discussing ethically engaged knowledge, not whether one can know a route and decline to walk it. Nor does it make practice blind. Reflection, deliberation, and learning remain forms of activity within the unfolding unity. Hard cases persist: weakness of will, trauma, institutional constraint, and conflicting duties complicate the claim that failure to act proves failure to know. Wang’s formula works best as a diagnostic of self-deception and moral formation, not a complete contemporary psychology of every action.',
  ], ['zhu-xi'], ['Instructions for Practical Living']),
  section('great-learning-gewu', 'The Great Learning and rectifying affairs', [
    'Wang reread the Great Learning against the curriculum identified with Zhu Xi. Where Zhu’s gewu emphasized investigating things to extend knowledge of their patterns, Wang understood the key phrase as rectifying affairs: setting right the concrete matters toward which intention is directed. Knowledge is extended by fully enacting pure knowing in those affairs. The dispute concerns where inquiry begins and what counts as completion. Wang feared that accumulating textual and external investigation could postpone transformation; Zhu’s defenders could answer that untested inward certainty mistakes desire for principle.',
    'The famous bamboo episode dramatizes the disagreement. According to later biographical tradition, Wang tried to investigate bamboo according to what he took Zhu’s method to require, became ill, and concluded that sagehood could not depend on exhausting external things. It should not be used as a transparent transcript of Zhu Xi’s instructions, which included reading, relationships, and practical affairs. As a retrospective teaching story, however, it captures Wang’s protest against an alienated learner who looks everywhere except at the intention through which a situation is already being met.',
  ], ['zhu-xi'], ['Great Learning', 'Inquiry on the Great Learning']),
  section('cultivation-affairs', 'Cultivation amid family, office, danger, and routine', [
    'Wang rejected cultivation that waits for a perfectly quiet setting. The heart-mind must be tested in family duties, administration, teaching, grief, conflict, and ordinary work. Quiet-sitting can collect attention, especially for beginners, but it becomes harmful if treated as an escape from affairs or a search for blankness. Learners examine intentions continuously, practice appropriate conduct, and extend pure knowing in each encounter. This emphasis explains why letters and conversations matter: moral training occurs in specific difficulties, not only through assent to universal propositions.',
    '“Removing selfish desire” likewise needs care. Wang does not require eliminating embodiment or every preference. He targets desires that narrow concern and manufacture excuses against what pure knowing registers. Yet the rhetoric can be abused if obedience to a ruler, parent, or teacher is declared the voice of principle. Wang’s own public service shows both the seriousness and the danger of action-centered cultivation. Institutions shape which options appear possible. Personal sincerity is not sufficient evidence that coercion is humane, so contemporary appropriation must join examination of intention to examination of power and consequence.',
  ], ['wang-yangming'], ['Instructions for Practical Living']),
  section('four-sentences', 'The Four Sentences and the Tianquan dispute', [
    'A late teaching known as the Four Sentences links four claims: the heart-mind’s original substance is described as beyond fixed good and evil; intentions display good and evil; pure knowing discerns them; and cultivation rectifies affairs by doing good and removing evil. The formulation is associated with a 1527 discussion at Tianquan Bridge involving Wang and the disciples Qian Dehong and Wang Ji. Its textual record and interpretation are contested. The first claim especially can sound inconsistent with Wang’s moral realism unless “beyond” means prior to partialized intention rather than morally indifferent.',
    'Later shorthand contrasts Qian’s “four haves,” which stresses the practical sequence of moral effort, with Wang Ji’s “four nothings,” which stresses the original nonattachment of mind, intention, knowing, and affairs. Wang’s reported response has been read as assigning different teachings to different learners or holding sudden insight and gradual cultivation together. Tu Weiming and Guo Tan offer distinct scholarly reconstructions; neither disciple’s slogan should simply replace Wang’s corpus. The dispute reveals a structural tension: how can innate moral capacity be originally complete while disciplined correction remains indispensable?',
  ], ['wang-yangming'], ['Instructions for Practical Living']),
  section('zhu-buddhist-labels', 'Zhu Xi, Lu Jiuyuan, and Buddhist comparison', [
    'Wang’s criticism of Zhu Xi is substantial but not a rejection of Confucian learning. He accepted the classics, revered sages, and shared the goal of forming humane conduct. He objected to a method that, as practiced, seemed to locate pattern outside the heart-mind and divide knowing from transformation. Later histories connect him with Lu Jiuyuan under the Lu–Wang “school of mind.” The comparison is illuminating because Lu also identified mind and pattern, but it should not imply simple institutional succession or identical arguments across three centuries.',
    'Contemporaries and later critics accused Wang’s inward emphasis of resembling Chan Buddhism, especially when radical disciples spoke of original mind and sudden realization. Wang himself criticized Buddhist withdrawal and insisted that pure knowing appears in ethical relationships. Similarity and rivalry can coexist: Song–Ming Confucian discourse developed after centuries of Buddhist philosophy, and polemical boundaries do not eliminate conceptual exchange. Calling Wang a disguised Buddhist dismisses his classical commitments; denying any Buddhist resonance sanitizes the intellectual world. Specific doctrines and practices must be compared rather than assigning ownership through labels.',
  ], ['zhu-xi', 'mencius'], ['Instructions for Practical Living']),
  section('reception-political-risk', 'Diverse receptions and the politics of moral certainty', [
    'Wang’s disciples developed rival emphases, and his prestige rose and fell after his death. The Ming court eventually granted honors, while critics blamed parts of the movement for undisciplined subjectivism. Wang learning traveled to Japan and Korea through distinct institutions and debates; later Japanese admirers associated it with resolute action, but no direct line makes his philosophy the cause of every activist or nationalist appropriation. Reception history must identify intermediaries, translations, political settings, and selective readings rather than treating “the school” as one actor.',
    'The enduring attraction is clear: morality cannot be outsourced to specialists, and genuine understanding must transform conduct. The danger is equally clear: a leader may baptize conviction as pure knowing and dismiss evidence or dissent as selfish obstruction. Wang’s own account provides internal checks—continuous self-examination, concern forming one body, testing in affairs, and correction of desire—but whether they suffice is disputed. A responsible modern reading pairs moral agency with fallibility, public reasons, consequences, and institutional accountability. Unity of knowledge and action should intensify responsibility, not grant immunity from criticism.',
  ], ['zhu-xi'], ['Instructions for Practical Living']),
  section('reading-path', 'A reading path from practice to contested legacy', [
    'Begin with selections from Instructions for Practical Living on knowing and acting, pure knowing, and mind as pattern, followed by the Inquiry on the Great Learning. Keep a ledger of speaker, audience, and practical problem; do not turn every reply into an abstract axiom. Use the current SEP entry to reconstruct chronology and intellectual context. Then compare Wang’s rereading of the Great Learning with Zhu Xi’s commentary. The question is not which thinker values action—both do—but how each diagnoses the gap between classical language, moral discernment, and reliable conduct.',
    'Next study the late Four Sentences through the primary record and both Tu Weiming’s and Guo Tan’s analyses. Test how Qian Dehong and Wang Ji relate original moral capacity to gradual correction. Ivanhoe’s ethical study then places Wang beside Mencius and Buddhist-inflected cultivation debates. Finish with reception cases kept locally specific rather than one global “school of mind.” Throughout, preserve four distinctions: innate capacity versus infallible impulse, unity versus causal automatism, later lineage label versus earlier doctrine, and energetic action versus justified action. Those controls keep an inspirational philosophy open to historical and ethical scrutiny.',
  ], ['zhu-xi', 'mencius'], ['Instructions for Practical Living', 'Inquiry on the Great Learning']),
];

